The circumference of the base of any cone is $2 \pi r$, so let $\phi$ be the radius of the base of the cone and then the circumference of the base of the cone is $2 \pi \phi$, and because we are cutting out a $252^{\circ}$ sector of a circle of radius 10, then the length of that arc is precisely $252^{\circ} /(360^{\circ}) \cdot (2 \pi) (10)=14 \pi$.
Hence, the circumference of the base of the cone is $14 \pi,$ so the base radius of the cone is $\frac{14 \pi}{2 \pi}=7$. We now know the slant height of the cone is 10, let $h$ be the height of the cone and the Pythagorean theorem gives :
\begin{align*}
10^2&=7^2+h^2\\
h &=3\sqrt{5}
\end{align*}
so height of the cone $=3\sqrt{5}.$</think>
